consiSt("nc",{"courts":[{"l":"HIGHEST COURT","n":"Supreme Court of North Carolina","d":"A Chief Justice and six associate justices, elected to eight-year terms; four make a quorum. Sits in Raleigh. Hears direct appeals in Business Court cases and class-certification rulings, appeals of right on substantial constitutional questions, and cases it certifies for discretionary review (G.S. 7A-10, 7A-27(a), 7A-30, 7A-31)."},{"l":"INTERMEDIATE","n":"North Carolina Court of Appeals","d":"Fifteen judges elected to eight-year terms, sitting in panels of three and en banc when a majority votes. Hears most appeals of right from the superior and district courts and many agency appeals (G.S. 7A-16, 7A-27(b))."},{"l":"TRIAL COURTS","n":"Superior Court (including the North Carolina Business Court)","d":"General-jurisdiction trial court; the proper division for civil actions over $25,000 (G.S. 7A-243). Special Superior Court Judges for Complex Business Cases hear cases designated as mandatory or discretionary complex business cases (G.S. 7A-45.4). Facial challenges to acts of the General Assembly go to a three-judge panel in Wake County (Rule 42(b)(4); G.S. 1-267.1)."},{"l":"LIMITED JURISDICTION","n":"District Court and magistrates","d":"District Court is the proper division for civil actions of $25,000 or less and for family matters (G.S. 7A-243). Magistrates hear assigned small claims up to $10,000 (G.S. 7A-210). The clerk of superior court handles estates and special proceedings."}],"slapp":[{"t":"No anti-SLAPP statute","d":"North Carolina has no anti-SLAPP law: no special motion to dismiss, no discovery stay, and no speech-based fee-shifting. We searched the General Statutes chapters where such a law would be placed (Chapter 1, Civil Procedure; Chapter 1A, Rules of Civil Procedure; Chapter 1D, Punitive Damages; Chapter 6, Costs; Chapter 7A, Judicial Department; Chapter 99, Libel and Slander) for “public participation” and “SLAPP” and found nothing. We did not search the whole General Statutes, so confirm on ncleg.gov before relying on this.\nA defendant sued over speech has only general tools. These are a Rule 12(b)(6) motion; mandatory Rule 11 sanctions; and G.S. 6-21.5 fees, which the court may award to a prevailing party when the loser’s pleading raised a complete absence of a justiciable issue of law or fact (findings required; a good-faith argument to change the law is protected). Libel claims against newspapers, periodicals, radio and television stations have their own rules in G.S. 99-1 and 99-2: the plaintiff must give pre-suit notice, and a timely retraction limits damages.","c":"Searched N.C.G.S. chs. 1, 1A, 1D, 6, 7A, 99 (none found); see N.C.G.S. § 6-21.5; N.C. R. Civ. P. 11(a)","v":true}],"oos":[{"t":"Pro hac vice is statutory: G.S. 84-4.1, $225, resident local counsel, reciprocity","d":"An attorney domiciled in another state, admitted and in good standing there, and retained by a party, may move the forum (the trial court, Industrial Commission, Utilities Commission, OAH, or an agency) for admission for that proceeding only. The motion, signed by the attorney, must include: name, bar number and status; the mailing address, phone and email to be used for Rule 5 service; a client-signed statement giving the client’s address and confirming the retention; an agreement to stay in the case until final determination unless allowed to withdraw and to submit to the courts’ and State Bar’s discipline and civil jurisdiction; a statement that the home state grants like privileges to North Carolina lawyers; a statement that the attorney has associated with, and is personally appearing with, a North Carolina resident attorney admitted here, who will file a registration statement with the State Bar and on whom service may be made; a disclosure of all public discipline and any pro hac vice revocations; and a $225 fee to the clerk ($200 to AOC, $25 to the State Bar). Even full compliance leaves admission to the court’s discretion.","c":"N.C.G.S. § 84-4.1(1)–(7)","v":true},{"t":"Admission can be revoked summarily","d":"Pro hac vice permission may be summarily revoked by the court or agency on its own motion and in its discretion; no hearing standard appears in the statute.","c":"N.C.G.S. § 84-4.2","v":true},{"t":"Temporary practice without admission (RPC 5.5)","d":"A lawyer admitted and not suspended elsewhere in the U.S. may practice temporarily in North Carolina if the lawyer is authorized to appear before a tribunal (or expects to be), the matter is reasonably related to home-state practice, the matter relates to arbitration or mediation, or the lawyer works with an actively participating North Carolina lawyer. Pro hac vice-type services are excluded unless admitted. In-house lawyers and lawyers practicing only federal or home-state law may have an office here. Rule 5.5(e) adds a North Carolina-specific option: a lawyer with a pending comity application who is domiciled here, supervised by a North Carolina firm lawyer, and has given written notice to the State Bar secretary may practice until the application is denied.","c":"N.C. R. Prof’l Conduct 5.5(b)–(e)","v":true},{"t":"Admission by comity: $2,000 and 4 of the last 6 years","d":"A lawyer licensed in an ‘approved jurisdiction’ (one whose conditions for admitting North Carolina lawyers without exam are not materially greater than North Carolina’s) may be admitted without the written bar exam, other than the MPRE. The lawyer must have actively and substantially practiced for at least four of the six years before applying, be in good standing everywhere licensed, pay a $2,000 fee (largely nonrefundable), and file character certificates, fingerprints, and transcripts.","c":"27 NCAC 03 .0502","v":true},{"t":"Limited-scope representation","d":"The Rules of Professional Conduct allow a lawyer to limit the scope of a representation if the limitation is reasonable. The collected texts do not include a court rule on limited appearances or withdrawal in the trial courts. Those are in the General Rules of Practice, which we could not collect, so check local practice before filing a limited appearance.","c":"N.C. R. Prof’l Conduct 1.2(c)","v":true},{"t":"E-filing and e-service","d":"Where e-filing is available in the county, attorneys must file under Rule 5 of the General Rules of Practice (the eCourts File & Serve system). The Supreme Court is authorized to require registration. Service on attorneys through the court’s e-filing system at the email of record is valid service, but it must be sent by 5:00 p.m. Eastern on a business day or it counts as served the next business day. Pro hac vice motions must give the email address to be used for Rule 5 service. The text of General Rule of Practice 5 and the e-filing rules was not collected because nccourts.gov blocked downloads.","c":"N.C. R. Civ. P. 3(a), 5(b), 5(e)(2); N.C.G.S. §§ 7A-49.5(b1), 84-4.1(1a)","v":true}],"ethics":[{"t":"Governing code: North Carolina Rules of Professional Conduct","d":"The State Bar’s Rules of Professional Conduct (27 NCAC 02) are approved by the Supreme Court; the current set dates from Supreme Court approval on July 24, 1997, with later amendments. They follow the ABA Model Rule numbering but have several real departures, below.","c":"N.C. R. Prof’l Conduct (History Notes, e.g., Rule 1.6)","v":true},{"t":"Broader permission to reveal a client’s crime","d":"Rule 1.6(b)(2) lets a lawyer reveal confidential information to prevent the commission of any crime by the client, not only crimes or frauds causing financial harm through the lawyer’s services as in the Model Rule. Rule 1.6(b)(4) separately covers preventing or rectifying a client’s crime or fraud in which the lawyer’s services were used.","c":"N.C. R. Prof’l Conduct 1.6(b)(2), (4)","v":true},{"t":"Fee disputes: mandatory notice of the State Bar program","d":"Before suing a client over a disputed fee, a lawyer must make reasonable efforts to tell the client about the State Bar’s fee dispute resolution program at least 30 days before filing, and must participate in good faith if the client uses it. Contingent fee agreements must be signed by the client and state how expenses are treated.","c":"N.C. R. Prof’l Conduct 1.5(c), (f)","v":true},{"t":"No humanitarian exception for financial assistance","d":"Rule 1.8(e) allows advancing court costs and litigation expenses and paying them for indigent clients. It contains no exception for modest living-expense gifts to indigent pro bono clients like Model Rule 1.8(e)(3).","c":"N.C. R. Prof’l Conduct 1.8(e)","v":true},{"t":"Separate rule banning sexual activity and sexual communications","d":"Rule 1.19 bars sexual activity and sexual communications with a client (including prospective clients until the lawyer declines and an organizational client’s representatives), and bars demanding them as a condition of representation. Pre-existing consensual relationships are excepted. The Model Rule equivalent, 1.8(j), does not address communications.","c":"N.C. R. Prof’l Conduct 1.19","v":true},{"t":"No anti-harassment rule; extra ‘prejudice the client’ rule","d":"Rule 8.4 has no counterpart to Model Rule 8.4(g) on harassment or discrimination. Instead, North Carolina’s 8.4(g) makes it misconduct to intentionally prejudice or damage a client during the representation (except as Rule 3.3 requires).","c":"N.C. R. Prof’l Conduct 8.4","v":true},{"t":"Detailed trust-account rules","d":"Rules 1.15-1 to 1.15-4 set detailed trust accounting duties. Each general trust account must be reconciled to the bank statement monthly, and a three-way reconciliation (general ledger, client ledgers, adjusted bank balance) must be done at least quarterly. The lawyer must sign and date the reconciliations and keep them for six years. Monthly and quarterly reviews of statements and checks are also required.","c":"N.C. R. Prof’l Conduct 1.15-3(d), (e), (h)","v":true},{"t":"Local customs and courtesy are enforceable","d":"Rule 3.5(a)(4) treats as conduct intended to disrupt a tribunal failing to follow known local customs of courtesy or practice without giving opposing counsel timely notice, undignified or discourteous conduct degrading to the tribunal, and intentional or habitual violation of procedure or evidence rules.","c":"N.C. R. Prof’l Conduct 3.5(a)(4)","v":true}],"style":"State of North Carolina","home":"https://www.nccourts.gov/","unique":{"mines":[{"t":"Serve within 60 days or keep the summons chain alive","d":"Personal service must be made within 60 days after the summons issues. To keep the case alive against an unserved defendant, get an endorsement or an alias or pluries summons within 90 days of the last one. If the chain breaks, the action is discontinued as to that defendant and, if revived, counts as commenced only on the new date. That can blow the statute of limitations.","c":"N.C. R. Civ. P. 4(c)–(e)"},{"t":"Do not plead a dollar figure in negligence or punitive claims","d":"In negligence actions and punitive damage claims over $25,000, the complaint must say only that damages exceed $25,000. The defendant may demand a written statement of the amount, due within 30 days.","c":"N.C. R. Civ. P. 8(a)(2)"},{"t":"Jury demand: 10 days after the last pleading","d":"Serve and file a written jury demand within 10 days after service of the last pleading on the issue (federal: 14). Missing it waives the jury.","c":"N.C. R. Civ. P. 38(b), (d)"},{"t":"Post-trial motions in 10 days, no extensions","d":"JNOV, motions to amend findings, and new-trial or alter-or-amend motions are due 10 days after entry of judgment (federal: 28). Neither the court nor a stipulation can extend these deadlines. The usual 30-day stipulated extension under Rule 6(b) does not apply.","c":"N.C. R. Civ. P. 6(b), 50(b), 52(b), 59(b), (e)"},{"t":"Requests for admission can come with the summons","d":"A plaintiff may serve requests for admission with the summons and complaint. They are deemed admitted unless answered within 30 days after service, except that a defendant gets at least 60 days from service of the summons. Deemed admissions are conclusive unless withdrawn on motion.","c":"N.C. R. Civ. P. 36(a), (b); 4(b)"},{"t":"Voluntary dismissal: easy once, fatal twice, one-year refiling","d":"A plaintiff can dismiss by notice any time before resting. A second notice dismissal of the same claim is an adjudication on the merits. A timely action dismissed without prejudice may be refiled within one year, but the refiled case is stayed until costs from the first case are paid.","c":"N.C. R. Civ. P. 41(a)(1), (d)"},{"t":"Pro hac vice needs out-of-state domicile, reciprocity and a resident co-counsel","d":"Only an attorney domiciled in another state qualifies. The motion must state that the home state grants like privileges to North Carolina lawyers and that a North Carolina resident attorney is associated and personally appearing. It must include a client-signed retention statement and the $225 fee. The court keeps discretion and may revoke summarily.","c":"N.C.G.S. §§ 84-4.1, 84-4.2"},{"t":"Business Court designation: 30 days, and waiver","d":"A defendant who wants Business Court must file the Notice of Designation within 30 days after receiving service of the pleading. A party opposing designation has 30 days after service of the notice and waives any ground not asserted.","c":"N.C.G.S. § 7A-45.4(d)(3), (e)"},{"t":"Appeal to the right court","d":"Final judgments in designated business cases, interlocutory Business Court orders affecting a substantial right, and every Rule 23 class-certification ruling are appealed directly to the Supreme Court, not the Court of Appeals.","c":"N.C.G.S. § 7A-27(a)(2)–(4)"},{"t":"A Court of Appeals dissent no longer gets you to the Supreme Court","d":"The appeal of right based on a dissent was repealed effective July 1, 2023. Without a substantial constitutional question, further review requires a petition for discretionary review.","c":"N.C.G.S. §§ 7A-30, 7A-31"},{"t":"Rule 9(j) certification in medical malpractice","d":"A malpractice complaint is dismissed unless it asserts that an expert reasonably expected to qualify under Rule 702 reviewed the care and records and will testify that the standard was breached, or it pleads res ipsa loquitur. A 120-day limitations extension is available only by motion before the statute runs.","c":"N.C. R. Civ. P. 9(j)"},{"t":"Rule 11 sanctions are mandatory, with no safe harbor","d":"If a paper is signed in violation of Rule 11, the court ‘shall impose’ an appropriate sanction on the signer, the party, or both. The text has no 21-day withdrawal window like the federal rule.","c":"N.C. R. Civ. P. 11(a)"}],"judge":[{"t":"No peremptory challenge of a judge","d":"No statute or civil rule we collected gives a party a peremptory challenge or automatic substitution of a judge. The Rules of Civil Procedure and General Statutes Chapters 1 and 7A were searched. Disqualification is for cause.","c":"N.C.G.S. chs. 1, 1A, 7A (searched)","v":true},{"t":"Recusal for cause: Code of Judicial Conduct","d":"Motions to recuse are governed by the North Carolina Code of Judicial Conduct (Canon 3C) and case law. That code was not collected because nccourts.gov blocked downloads, so the grounds, timing, and whether another judge must hear the motion are not stated here.","c":"N.C. Code of Jud. Conduct Canon 3C (not collected)","v":false},{"t":"When a judge steps aside on a motion","d":"A judge who disqualifies from a motion may refer it to a regular resident superior court judge of the district, or a judge regularly holding court there or in an adjoining district, who decides it as if presiding. If the trial judge becomes unable to act after verdict or hearing, the senior resident superior court judge (or chief district judge) may enter judgment or order a new trial.","c":"N.C.G.S. § 7A-49.1; N.C. R. Civ. P. 63","v":true}],"speech":[{"t":"Media libel: pre-suit notice and retraction","d":"Before suing a newspaper or periodical for libel, or a radio or television station for defamation, the plaintiff must serve written notice at least 5 days before filing, identifying the statements claimed to be false. If the publication was in good faith from an honest mistake on reasonable grounds and a full correction, apology and retraction runs within 10 days after the notice, the plaintiff recovers only actual damages. These rules do not apply to anonymous publications. Broadcasters are liable for third-party statements only if negligent.","c":"N.C.G.S. §§ 99-1, 99-2, 99-3, 99-5","v":true},{"t":"State constitutional speech protection","d":"Whether the North Carolina Constitution protects speech more broadly than the First Amendment is not addressed here. The constitution and case law were not collected.","c":"Not collected","v":false}],"other":[{"t":"Business Court designation","d":"Corporate, securities, antitrust (beyond G.S. 75-1.1), trademark, IP, and trade secret disputes may be designated mandatory complex business cases, as may contract cases between entities of $1 million or more if all parties consent. Cases of $5 million or more in those categories must be designated. Defendants designate within 30 days after service of the pleading, and any opposition is due within 30 days after service of the notice, with unasserted grounds waived. Final judgments go straight to the Supreme Court.","c":"N.C.G.S. §§ 7A-45.4(a), (b), (d), (e), 7A-27(a)(2)","v":true},{"t":"Contributory negligence remains a pleading issue","d":"Contributory negligence is a listed affirmative defense. When it is pleaded, the plaintiff may serve a reply alleging last clear chance, the one reply allowed without a court order besides replies to counterclaims.","c":"N.C. R. Civ. P. 7(a), 8(c)","v":true}]},"appeals":{"asof":"November 10, 2026","src":"N.C.G.S. §§ 1-269 to 1-294 (Chapter 1, Article 27, Appeal) and §§ 7A-27, 7A-30, 7A-31, 7A-32, 7A-45.4 (Chapter 7A), official ncleg.gov compilations (2026); N.C. Rules of Civil Procedure, N.C.G.S. § 1A-1, Rules 50, 52, 54, 58, 59, 60, 62. The North Carolina Rules of Appellate Procedure (N.C. R. App. P.), which set most appellate deadlines, could NOT be collected: nccourts.gov returned HTTP 403 to automated requests. Every point that rests on the appellate rules is marked as not verified. Summaries are Consi’s.","intro":{"t":"Appeals in North Carolina: statutes decide where and what; the appellate rules decide when and how","d":"Most civil appeals go from the superior or district court to the 15-judge Court of Appeals, which sits in panels of three. Some go straight to the Supreme Court: final judgments in designated complex business cases, certain interlocutory orders of a Business Court judge, and any trial court ruling on class certification under Rule 23 (G.S. 7A-27(a)).\nSince July 1, 2023, a dissent in the Court of Appeals no longer creates an appeal of right to the Supreme Court; the only appeal of right left from the Court of Appeals is for a substantial constitutional question (G.S. 7A-30). Everything else needs discretionary review under G.S. 7A-31.\nThe statute on notice of appeal (G.S. 1-279.1) simply points to the Rules of Appellate Procedure for timing and manner. Those rules were not collected for this package, so all appellate-rule deadlines below are flagged for verification."},"groups":[["Which court hears the appeal",[{"t":"Default: Court of Appeals","d":"Appeal lies of right to the Court of Appeals from any final judgment of a superior court (including review of agency decisions) and any final judgment of a district court in a civil action, unless G.S. 7A-27(a) sends it to the Supreme Court.","c":"N.C.G.S. § 7A-27(b)(1)–(2)"},{"t":"Direct to the Supreme Court: Business Court and class certification","d":"Appeal goes directly to the Supreme Court from (1) any final judgment in a mandatory complex business case under G.S. 7A-45.4 or a discretionary complex business case under General Rule of Practice 2.1; (2) any interlocutory order of a Business Court judge that affects a substantial right, in effect determines the action, discontinues it, or grants or refuses a new trial; and (3) any trial court decision on class certification under Rule 23. A party who disagrees with a ruling on a Business Court designation opposition also appeals under 7A-27(a).","c":"N.C.G.S. §§ 7A-27(a)(2)–(4), 7A-45.4(e)"},{"t":"No more dissent-based appeals of right","d":"G.S. 7A-30(2), which gave an appeal of right when a Court of Appeals panel had a dissent, was repealed effective July 1, 2023. The remaining appeal of right is from a Court of Appeals decision that directly involves a substantial question under the U.S. or North Carolina Constitution.","c":"N.C.G.S. § 7A-30 (as amended by S.L. 2023-134, s. 16.21(d))"}]],["What can be appealed before final judgment",[{"t":"Substantial-right interlocutory appeals","d":"An interlocutory order is immediately appealable to the Court of Appeals if it affects a substantial right, in effect determines the action and prevents a judgment, discontinues the action, or grants or refuses a new trial. The statute also lists orders on claims under G.S. 50-19.1, temporary injunctions against enforcing an act of the General Assembly (when the State or a subdivision is a party), and denial of transfer to a three-judge panel under Rule 42(b)(4).","c":"N.C.G.S. §§ 1-277(a), 7A-27(b)(3)"},{"t":"Personal jurisdiction rulings: appeal now or preserve","d":"Any interested party may immediately appeal an adverse ruling on jurisdiction over the person or property of the defendant, or may preserve the objection for a later appeal.","c":"N.C.G.S. § 1-277(b)"},{"t":"Rule 54(b) certification","d":"A judgment on fewer than all claims or parties is final and appealable only if the court determines in the judgment that there is no just reason for delay; otherwise it remains revisable and is not appealable except as rules or statutes expressly provide (for example, substantial-right orders).","c":"N.C. R. Civ. P. 54(b)"},{"t":"As-applied challenges to acts of the General Assembly","d":"A party has an immediate right to appeal an order granting or denying interlocutory, temporary, or permanent injunctive or declaratory relief restraining the State or a subdivision from enforcing an act as applied, or denying a motion to stay such an injunction. It does not apply to facial challenges heard by a three-judge panel.","c":"N.C. R. Civ. P. 62(h)"},{"t":"Certiorari and other writs","d":"The Court of Appeals and Supreme Court may issue certiorari, mandamus, prohibition, and supersedeas to supervise trial courts; when used as a substitute for an appeal, certiorari issues on an undertaking for costs only. The petition procedure is in the appellate rules (Rule 21 for certiorari, Rule 23 for supersedeas and temporary stays). Not verified: the Rules of Appellate Procedure could not be downloaded (nccourts.gov blocked automated requests); check the current codification before relying on this.","c":"N.C.G.S. §§ 1-269, 7A-32(b)–(c); N.C. R. App. P. 21, 23 (not verified)"}]],["Notice of appeal, tolling, and cross-appeals",[{"t":"Time and manner come from the appellate rules","d":"A party entitled to appeal in a civil action or special proceeding appeals by giving notice of appeal within the time, in the manner, and with the effect provided in the Rules of Appellate Procedure. Under Appellate Rule 3(c), the notice is due within 30 days after entry of judgment if the judgment is served within the 3 days Rule 58 requires, or within 30 days after service if it is not. Not verified: the Rules of Appellate Procedure could not be downloaded (nccourts.gov blocked automated requests); check the current codification before relying on this.","c":"N.C.G.S. § 1-279.1; N.C. R. App. P. 3(c) (not verified)"},{"t":"Entry and service of the judgment start the clocks","d":"A judgment is entered when it is written, signed by the judge, and filed with the clerk. The designated party (or the drafter) must serve it on all parties within 3 days. Until it is served, the time for Rule 50(b), 52(b), and 59 motions is tolled, but never more than 90 days from entry. Add 3 days if served by mail.","c":"N.C. R. Civ. P. 58"},{"t":"Post-trial motions: 10 days, and they suspend the appeal time","d":"JNOV (Rule 50(b)), motions to amend findings (Rule 52(b)), and new-trial or alter-or-amend motions (Rule 59) are all due within 10 days after entry of judgment, and neither parties nor the court may extend these times. Under Appellate Rule 3(c)(3), a timely motion under these rules tolls the time to appeal for all parties until the order disposing of it is entered and served. A Rule 60(b) motion does not affect finality or suspend the judgment, so it does not toll the appeal time.","c":"N.C. R. Civ. P. 6(b), 50(b), 52(b), 59(b), (e), 60(b); N.C. R. App. P. 3(c)(3) (not verified)"},{"t":"Cross-appeals","d":"Any aggrieved party may appeal or cross-appeal; ‘party aggrieved’ includes a party challenging the grant or denial of a motion under the Rules of Civil Procedure. Under Appellate Rule 3(c), once one party timely files and serves a notice of appeal, any other party may file and serve a notice within 10 days after the first notice was served on it. Not verified: the Rules of Appellate Procedure could not be downloaded (nccourts.gov blocked automated requests); check the current codification before relying on this.","c":"N.C.G.S. § 1-271; N.C. R. App. P. 3(c) (not verified)"},{"t":"Stays and bonds","d":"No execution may issue until the time to notice an appeal expires, except injunction and receivership judgments. To stay a money judgment on appeal, the appellant posts an undertaking set by the court after notice and hearing (considering the judgment, insurance limits, and net worth); for judgments of $25 million or more the bond is capped at $25 million unless the appellee proves the appellant is dissipating, hiding, or moving assets. A perfected appeal stays proceedings on the matters embraced in the judgment; the court may dispense with or limit security for fiduciary appellants, including limiting it to $50,000. The State and local governments need no bond.","c":"N.C. R. Civ. P. 62(a), (d), (e); N.C.G.S. §§ 1-289(a1)–(c), 1-294"}]],["Record, briefs, and limits (appellate rules, not verified)",[{"t":"Transcript and record on appeal","d":"In North Carolina, the parties assemble and settle a printed record on appeal in the trial court rather than transmitting the trial file. Under Appellate Rules 7, 11 and 12, the appellant must arrange the transcript within 14 days after filing the notice of appeal, serve a proposed record within 35 days after the transcript is delivered (or after notice of appeal if there is no transcript), and file the settled record in the appellate court within 15 days after it is settled. Confirm every interval against the current rules. Not verified: the Rules of Appellate Procedure could not be downloaded (nccourts.gov blocked automated requests); check the current codification before relying on this.","c":"N.C. R. App. P. 7, 11, 12 (not verified)"},{"t":"Briefing schedule","d":"Under Appellate Rule 13, the appellant’s brief is due 30 days after the clerk mails the printed record, and the appellee’s brief 30 days after service of the appellant’s brief. A reply brief may be filed within 14 days after service of the appellee’s brief (Rule 28(h)). Not verified: the Rules of Appellate Procedure could not be downloaded (nccourts.gov blocked automated requests); check the current codification before relying on this.","c":"N.C. R. App. P. 13, 28(h) (not verified)"},{"t":"Word limits and required contents","d":"Appellate Rule 28(j) sets word limits for Court of Appeals briefs (8,750 words for principal briefs and 3,750 for replies, with a certificate of compliance). Rule 28(b)(4) requires a statement of the grounds for appellate review, which for an interlocutory appeal must show the substantial right or certification. Issues not presented and argued in the brief are abandoned (Rule 28(b)(6)); preservation of error is governed by Rule 10. Not verified: the Rules of Appellate Procedure could not be downloaded (nccourts.gov blocked automated requests); check the current codification before relying on this.","c":"N.C. R. App. P. 10, 28(b), (j) (not verified)"}]],["Rehearing and Supreme Court review",[{"t":"Discretionary review (PDR)","d":"The Supreme Court may certify a case for review before or after the Court of Appeals decides it. Before decision: significant public interest, legal principles of major significance, likely delay causing substantial harm, appellate workload, or the court system’s jurisdiction and integrity. After decision: significant public interest, major legal significance, or likely conflict with a Supreme Court decision. Interlocutory Court of Appeals rulings (including remands for new trial) are certified only if delay would probably cause substantial harm.","c":"N.C.G.S. § 7A-31(a)–(c)"},{"t":"Timing of PDRs and notices of appeal to the Supreme Court","d":"Under Appellate Rules 14 and 15, a notice of appeal under G.S. 7A-30 and a petition for discretionary review after decision are filed within 15 days after the Court of Appeals’ mandate issues; the mandate ordinarily issues 20 days after the opinion is filed (Rule 32). Rules 14, 15, and 16 were reported amended effective April 1, 2025; the effect of that amendment was not checked. Not verified: the Rules of Appellate Procedure could not be downloaded (nccourts.gov blocked automated requests); check the current codification before relying on this.","c":"N.C. R. App. P. 14, 15, 32 (not verified)"},{"t":"Rehearing","d":"Appellate Rule 31 governs petitions for rehearing, filed within 15 days after the mandate issues; whether and when rehearing is available in each court should be checked in the current rule. Not verified: the Rules of Appellate Procedure could not be downloaded (nccourts.gov blocked automated requests); check the current codification before relying on this.","c":"N.C. R. App. P. 31 (not verified)"}]],["Electronic filing",[{"t":"Appellate e-filing site","d":"Filings in the Supreme Court and Court of Appeals are made through the appellate courts’ electronic filing site, ncappellatecourts.org, which requires registration. Appellate Rule 26 governs filing and service. Trial-court e-filing uses eCourts File & Serve under General Rule of Practice 5, which the Supreme Court adopts under G.S. 7A-49.5. Not verified: the Rules of Appellate Procedure could not be downloaded (nccourts.gov blocked automated requests); check the current codification before relying on this.","c":"N.C.G.S. § 7A-49.5; N.C. R. App. P. 26 (not verified); www.ncappellatecourts.org"}]],["Deadlines",[{"t":"Serve signed judgment on all parties","d":"3 days from entry of judgment (written, signed, filed).\nFailure tolls Rule 50(b), 52(b) and 59 periods, but not beyond 90 days from entry.","c":"N.C. R. Civ. P. 58","due":{"n":3,"from":"the trigger date (entry of judgment)","note":"Failure tolls Rule 50(b), 52(b) and 59 periods, but not beyond 90 days from entry."}},{"t":"JNOV, motion to amend findings, new-trial or alter/amend motion","d":"10 days from entry of judgment.\nCannot be extended by the court or by stipulation (Rule 6(b)). Add 3 days if the judgment was served by mail (Rule 58).","c":"N.C. R. Civ. P. 50(b), 52(b), 59(b), (e)","due":{"n":10,"from":"the trigger date (entry of judgment)","note":"Cannot be extended by the court or by stipulation (Rule 6(b)). Add 3 days if the judgment was served by mail (Rule 58)."}},{"t":"Notice of appeal, civil case","d":"30 days from entry of judgment if served within 3 days under Rule 58; otherwise service of the judgment.\nAppellate-rule deadline NOT verified against collected text (nccourts.gov blocked). Tolled by a timely Rule 50(b), 52(b) or 59 motion under App. R. 3(c)(3).","c":"N.C.G.S. § 1-279.1; N.C. R. App. P. 3(c)","due":{"n":30,"from":"the trigger date (entry of judgment if served within 3 days under Rule 58; otherwise service of the judgment)","note":"Appellate-rule deadline NOT verified against collected text (nccourts.gov blocked). Tolled by a timely Rule 50(b), 52(b) or 59 motion under App. R. 3(c)(3)."}},{"t":"Cross-appeal notice","d":"10 days from service of the first notice of appeal.\nNOT verified against collected text.","c":"N.C. R. App. P. 3(c)","due":{"n":10,"from":"the trigger date (service of the first notice of appeal)","note":"NOT verified against collected text."}},{"t":"Contract for transcript","d":"14 days from filing of notice of appeal.\nNOT verified against collected text.","c":"N.C. R. App. P. 7","due":{"n":14,"from":"the trigger date (filing of notice of appeal)","note":"NOT verified against collected text."}},{"t":"Serve proposed record on appeal","d":"35 days from delivery of transcript (or filing of notice of appeal if no transcript).\nNOT verified against collected text.","c":"N.C. R. App. P. 11","due":{"n":35,"from":"the trigger date (delivery of transcript)","note":"NOT verified against collected text."}},{"t":"Appellant’s brief (Court of Appeals)","d":"30 days from clerk’s mailing of the printed record.\nNOT verified against collected text.","c":"N.C. R. App. P. 13","due":{"n":30,"from":"the trigger date (clerk’s mailing of the printed record)","note":"NOT verified against collected text."}},{"t":"Appellee’s brief","d":"30 days from service of appellant’s brief.\nNOT verified against collected text.","c":"N.C. R. App. P. 13","due":{"n":30,"from":"the trigger date (service of appellant’s brief)","note":"NOT verified against collected text."}},{"t":"Reply brief","d":"14 days from service of appellee’s brief.\nNOT verified against collected text.","c":"N.C. R. App. P. 28(h)","due":{"n":14,"from":"the trigger date (service of appellee’s brief)","note":"NOT verified against collected text."}},{"t":"Notice of appeal (G.S. 7A-30) or petition for discretionary review to the Supreme Court","d":"15 days from issuance of the Court of Appeals’ mandate.\nRules NOT verified; Rules 14–16 reportedly amended effective April 1, 2025. Dissent-based appeals of right were repealed July 1, 2023.","c":"N.C.G.S. §§ 7A-30, 7A-31; N.C. R. App. P. 14, 15","due":{"n":15,"from":"the trigger date (issuance of the Court of Appeals’ mandate)","note":"Rules NOT verified; Rules 14–16 reportedly amended effective April 1, 2025. Dissent-based appeals of right were repealed July 1, 2023."}},{"t":"Rule 60(b)(1)–(3) motion","d":"365 days from entry of the judgment or order.\nDoes not toll the time to appeal or suspend the judgment.","c":"N.C. R. Civ. P. 60(b)","due":{"n":365,"from":"the trigger date (entry of the judgment or order)","note":"Does not toll the time to appeal or suspend the judgment."}}]]],"courts":[],"links":[["Official site","https://www.nccourts.gov/"]]},"rulesName":"North Carolina Rules of Civil Procedure","rulesSrc":"https://www.ncleg.gov/EnactedLegislation/Statutes/PDF/ByChapter/Chapter_1A.pdf","rulesAsof":"as checked November 10, 2026","rulesNote":"Each entry is our summary of the rule, not the rule itself. Tap Read the official text before you rely on it.","rules":[{"n":"1","t":"Scope of rules","s":"Govern civil actions and proceedings in the superior and district courts, except where a statute prescribes a different procedure, and tort actions before the Industrial Commission unless a statute differs.","u":"https://www.ncleg.gov/EnactedLegislation/Statutes/PDF/ByChapter/Chapter_1A.pdf"},{"n":"2","t":"One form of action","s":"There is one form of action, the civil action.","u":"https://www.ncleg.gov/EnactedLegislation/Statutes/PDF/ByChapter/Chapter_1A.pdf"},{"n":"3","t":"Commencement of action","s":"An action starts by filing a complaint, or by having the clerk issue a summons with an order allowing the complaint to be filed within 20 days; the action abates if the complaint is not filed in the time the order sets. This “summons and order” route has no federal counterpart; it commences the action before the complaint is filed. Where e-filing is available, attorneys must file under Rule 5 of the General Rules of Practice.","u":"https://www.ncleg.gov/EnactedLegislation/Statutes/PDF/ByChapter/Chapter_1A.pdf"},{"n":"4","t":"Process","s":"The clerk issues the summons on filing (in any event within 5 days); the summons tells the defendant to answer within 30 days. Personal or substituted service must be made within 60 days of issuance (federal: 90 days after filing), but the action can be kept alive by an endorsement or an alias or pluries summons obtained within 90 days of the last summons or endorsement (two years for service abroad and certain tax foreclosures); if the chain breaks, the action is discontinued as to unserved defendants and later counts as commenced only when a new summons issues or an endorsement is made. Service on individuals and entities may be by delivery, registered or certified mail, a designated delivery service, or USPS signature confirmation; publication (once a week for 3 weeks, 40 days to respond) only after due diligence; email service is expressly not authorized. Out-of-state servers must be non-parties at least 21 years old.","u":"https://www.ncleg.gov/EnactedLegislation/Statutes/PDF/ByChapter/Chapter_1A.pdf"},{"n":"5","t":"Service and filing of pleadings and other papers","s":"Later papers are served on the attorney of record, through the court’s e-filing system at the email of record, by email, by hand, by fax, or by mail; e-service, email and fax must go out by 5:00 p.m. Eastern on a business day or count as served the next business day, and mail service is complete on deposit. In superior court, briefs on dispositive motions must be served at least 2 days before the hearing. Papers must be filed before or within 5 days after service; discovery is not filed unless needed for a motion, agreed, or ordered. A certificate of service showing date, method, and address is required (the e-filing system’s automated certificate suffices).","u":"https://www.ncleg.gov/EnactedLegislation/Statutes/PDF/ByChapter/Chapter_1A.pdf"},{"n":"6","t":"Time","s":"Exclude the trigger day and include the last day unless it is a weekend or a legal holiday when the courthouse is closed; for periods under 7 days, weekends and holidays are excluded. Parties may stipulate, without a court order, to extensions totaling up to 30 days, but no one may extend the times under Rules 50(b), 52, 59(b), (d), (e), or 60(b) except as those rules allow. Motions and notices of hearing must be served at least 5 days before the hearing, and opposing affidavits at least 2 days before. Add 3 days when served by mail (5 days for Address Confidentiality Program participants on periods of 10 days or less).","u":"https://www.ncleg.gov/EnactedLegislation/Statutes/PDF/ByChapter/Chapter_1A.pdf"},{"n":"7","t":"Pleadings allowed; motions","s":"Pleadings are the complaint, answer, reply to a counterclaim, answer to a crossclaim, third-party complaint and answer; a reply alleging last clear chance is allowed when the answer pleads contributory negligence. Motions must be in writing with particular grounds unless made at a hearing or trial. In multicounty districts, a motion may be heard in another county of the district with the senior resident judge’s permission.","u":"https://www.ncleg.gov/EnactedLegislation/Statutes/PDF/ByChapter/Chapter_1A.pdf"},{"n":"8","t":"General rules of pleadings","s":"Notice pleading, but the claim must be “sufficiently particular” to give notice of the transactions and occurrences to be proved. In negligence actions and punitive-damage claims over $25,000, do not state a dollar demand; plead only that damages exceed $25,000 (any party may then request a written statement of the amount, due within 30 days). In business-type cases listed in G.S. 7A-45.4(a), the pleading must say whether damages of $5 million or more are sought. Contributory negligence and truth in defamation are among the listed affirmative defenses.","u":"https://www.ncleg.gov/EnactedLegislation/Statutes/PDF/ByChapter/Chapter_1A.pdf"},{"n":"9","t":"Pleading special matters","s":"Fraud, duress, and mistake must be pleaded with particularity; capacity of a non-natural party must be affirmatively alleged. Rule 9(j): a medical malpractice complaint is dismissed unless it certifies pre-filing review by a reasonably expected Rule 702 expert (or pleads res ipsa loquitur); a resident superior court judge may extend the limitations period up to 120 days to comply. Punitive damages must be specifically demanded with the aggravating factor pleaded with particularity (Rule 9(k)).","u":"https://www.ncleg.gov/EnactedLegislation/Statutes/PDF/ByChapter/Chapter_1A.pdf"},{"n":"10","t":"Form of pleadings","s":"Caption, numbered paragraphs, and separate counts; exhibits to a pleading are part of it for all purposes.","u":"https://www.ncleg.gov/EnactedLegislation/Statutes/PDF/ByChapter/Chapter_1A.pdf"},{"n":"11","t":"Signing and verification of pleadings","s":"Every paper must be signed by an attorney of record (with address) or the unrepresented party, certifying reasonable inquiry, factual and legal support, and no improper purpose. Unlike federal Rule 11, there is no 21-day safe harbor and sanctions are mandatory (“shall impose”) once a violation is found. Rules 11(b)–(d) govern verification by a party, agent, attorney, or corporate officer.","u":"https://www.ncleg.gov/EnactedLegislation/Statutes/PDF/ByChapter/Chapter_1A.pdf"},{"n":"12","t":"Defenses and objections","s":"Answer within 30 days after service of summons and complaint (federal: 21 days); replies to counterclaims and answers to crossclaims also 30 days. After a Rule 12 motion is decided, the responsive pleading is due in 20 days. The Rule 12(b) defenses track the federal list except that (7) is failure to join a “necessary” party; personal-jurisdiction, venue, process, and service defenses are waived if omitted from a pre-answer motion or the answer. After remand from federal court, a party has 30 days from filing of the certified remand order to plead.","u":"https://www.ncleg.gov/EnactedLegislation/Statutes/PDF/ByChapter/Chapter_1A.pdf"},{"n":"13","t":"Counterclaim and crossclaim","s":"Compulsory counterclaims arising from the same transaction or occurrence must be pleaded; permissive counterclaims are allowed. An omitted counterclaim may be added by amendment with leave for oversight, inadvertence, excusable neglect, or when justice requires.","u":"https://www.ncleg.gov/EnactedLegislation/Statutes/PDF/ByChapter/Chapter_1A.pdf"},{"n":"14","t":"Third-party practice","s":"A defendant may bring in a third party without leave if the third-party complaint is filed within 45 days after serving the answer (federal: 14 days); otherwise leave is needed. Special provisions allow the State to be made a third-party defendant in tort cases under Tort Claims Act limits.","u":"https://www.ncleg.gov/EnactedLegislation/Statutes/PDF/ByChapter/Chapter_1A.pdf"},{"n":"15","t":"Amended and supplemental pleadings","s":"A party may amend once as of course before a responsive pleading is served (or within 30 days if no responsive pleading is allowed and the case is not calendared); otherwise by leave, freely given, or written consent. Response to an amended pleading is due within 30 days. Relation back turns on whether the original pleading gave notice of the transactions or occurrences; the text has no separate rule for changing parties.","u":"https://www.ncleg.gov/EnactedLegislation/Statutes/PDF/ByChapter/Chapter_1A.pdf"},{"n":"16","t":"Pre-trial procedure","s":"The court may hold a pretrial conference and enter an order that controls the case. In medical malpractice actions, a final conference follows the close of discovery and consent trial-setting orders go to the senior resident or chief district judge for approval.","u":"https://www.ncleg.gov/EnactedLegislation/Statutes/PDF/ByChapter/Chapter_1A.pdf"},{"n":"17","t":"Parties plaintiff and defendant; capacity","s":"Actions must be prosecuted by the real party in interest, with a reasonable time to ratify, join, or substitute before dismissal. Minors and incompetents sue or defend through a general guardian or a guardian ad litem appointed under detailed procedures, including guardians ad litem for unborn persons and entities not yet in existence.","u":"https://www.ncleg.gov/EnactedLegislation/Statutes/PDF/ByChapter/Chapter_1A.pdf"},{"n":"18","t":"Joinder of claims and remedies","s":"Any number of claims may be joined; a money claim may be joined with a claim to set aside a fraudulent conveyance before judgment.","u":"https://www.ncleg.gov/EnactedLegislation/Statutes/PDF/ByChapter/Chapter_1A.pdf"},{"n":"19","t":"Necessary joinder of parties","s":"Persons “united in interest” must be joined; the court must order joinder of others when complete determination requires them. The pleader must name and explain omitted necessary persons. The Speaker of the House and President Pro Tempore of the Senate must be joined as defendants in any action challenging the validity of a North Carolina statute or constitutional provision.","u":"https://www.ncleg.gov/EnactedLegislation/Statutes/PDF/ByChapter/Chapter_1A.pdf"},{"n":"20","t":"Permissive joinder of parties","s":"Parties may join or be joined when claims arise from the same transaction or occurrence and share a common question; the court may order separate trials to prevent prejudice.","u":"https://www.ncleg.gov/EnactedLegislation/Statutes/PDF/ByChapter/Chapter_1A.pdf"},{"n":"21","t":"Misjoinder and nonjoinder","s":"Misjoinder is not a ground for dismissal; parties may be added or dropped and claims severed at any stage.","u":"https://www.ncleg.gov/EnactedLegislation/Statutes/PDF/ByChapter/Chapter_1A.pdf"},{"n":"22","t":"Interpleader","s":"Claimants exposing a party to multiple liability may be required to interplead; the court may order disputed funds deposited in an interest-bearing account or with the clerk.","u":"https://www.ncleg.gov/EnactedLegislation/Statutes/PDF/ByChapter/Chapter_1A.pdf"},{"n":"23","t":"Class actions","s":"Much shorter than the federal rule: a class suit is allowed when the class is too numerous to bring all before the court and the representatives will fairly insure adequate representation; it has no Rule 23(b) categories in the text. Derivative complaints must be verified; dismissal or compromise needs court approval and notice to the class; tax-refund class actions must follow G.S. 105-241.18. A class-certification decision is appealable directly to the Supreme Court (G.S. 7A-27(a)(4)).","u":"https://www.ncleg.gov/EnactedLegislation/Statutes/PDF/ByChapter/Chapter_1A.pdf"},{"n":"24","t":"Intervention","s":"Intervention of right (unconditional statutory right or impaired interest not adequately represented) and permissive intervention, on timely motion with a pleading attached. The Speaker and President Pro Tempore may intervene of right by filing a notice at any stage.","u":"https://www.ncleg.gov/EnactedLegislation/Statutes/PDF/ByChapter/Chapter_1A.pdf"},{"n":"25","t":"Substitution of parties","s":"On death, substitution may be ordered within the time for presenting estate claims under G.S. 28A-19-3; on adjudicated incompetency, within one year. The court may order abatement unless the action is continued within 6 to 12 months; no abatement after verdict.","u":"https://www.ncleg.gov/EnactedLegislation/Statutes/PDF/ByChapter/Chapter_1A.pdf"},{"n":"26","t":"General provisions governing discovery","s":"Scope is anything relevant to the subject matter that is not privileged and is reasonably calculated to lead to admissible evidence; the text has no federal-style proportionality test or mandatory initial disclosures. Expert disclosures (by optional written report or by interrogatory) are due at least 90 days before trial, rebuttal within 30 days after the other side’s disclosure; draft reports and most attorney–expert communications are protected. A party may request a discovery meeting no earlier than 40 days after the complaint is filed; it is held at least 21 days after the request, and a plan or joint report is due 14 days after the meeting. Asbestos plaintiffs must file a bankruptcy-trust statement within 30 days of filing. Medical malpractice discovery is scheduled by the court and ordinarily completed within 150 days.","u":"https://www.ncleg.gov/EnactedLegislation/Statutes/PDF/ByChapter/Chapter_1A.pdf"},{"n":"27","t":"Depositions before action or pending appeal","s":"A verified petition to perpetuate testimony must be served at least 20 days before the hearing; the trial court may also allow depositions pending appeal.","u":"https://www.ncleg.gov/EnactedLegislation/Statutes/PDF/ByChapter/Chapter_1A.pdf"},{"n":"28","t":"Persons before whom depositions may be taken","s":"Lists officers authorized in the U.S. and abroad and disqualifies relatives, employees, attorneys, financially interested persons, and reporters under “blanket contracts” with a party or counsel, unless waived by stipulation disclosed in the notice. Covers commissions for depositions in North Carolina for foreign proceedings.","u":"https://www.ncleg.gov/EnactedLegislation/Statutes/PDF/ByChapter/Chapter_1A.pdf"},{"n":"29","t":"Stipulations regarding discovery procedure","s":"Parties may stipulate in writing to deposition logistics and modify other discovery procedures unless the court orders otherwise.","u":"https://www.ncleg.gov/EnactedLegislation/Statutes/PDF/ByChapter/Chapter_1A.pdf"},{"n":"30","t":"Depositions upon oral examination","s":"Leave is needed for a plaintiff’s deposition within 30 days of service unless the defendant has sought discovery or special notice is given. Notice must be served at least 10 days ahead (15 days if any party resides out of state), and 15 more days when a Rule 34 request accompanies a party deposition (objections due 7 days before). Residents may be deposed only in their home, work, or business county; nonresidents only in their county or within 50 miles of service unless the court orders a forum-choosing party to appear locally. No federal-style 7-hour or 10-deposition limits appear in the text. The deponent has 30 days to review and sign.","u":"https://www.ncleg.gov/EnactedLegislation/Statutes/PDF/ByChapter/Chapter_1A.pdf"},{"n":"31","t":"Depositions upon written questions","s":"Cross questions within 30 days after service of the notice and questions; redirect within 10 days; recross within 10 days.","u":"https://www.ncleg.gov/EnactedLegislation/Statutes/PDF/ByChapter/Chapter_1A.pdf"},{"n":"32","t":"Use of depositions","s":"A deposition may be used for any purpose if the witness is dead, more than 100 miles from the place of trial, out of the U.S., unable to attend, unsubpoenable, or is an expert whose testimony was videotaped under Rule 30(b)(4). Rule 32(a)(2) also lets an adverse party use the deposition of a witness who testifies as substantive evidence.","u":"https://www.ncleg.gov/EnactedLegislation/Statutes/PDF/ByChapter/Chapter_1A.pdf"},{"n":"33","t":"Interrogatories","s":"No more than 50 interrogatories to any party, counting parts and subparts separately (federal: 25), unless leave or agreement. Answers are due in 30 days, but a defendant has 45 days after service of summons and complaint. Responses must follow each interrogatory in the space provided or restate it.","u":"https://www.ncleg.gov/EnactedLegislation/Statutes/PDF/ByChapter/Chapter_1A.pdf"},{"n":"34","t":"Production of documents, ESI, and things","s":"Responses are due in 30 days (defendant: 45 days after service of summons and complaint). Requests may specify the ESI form; objections must state reasons and the form the responder will use. Responses must be in the space provided or restate the request.","u":"https://www.ncleg.gov/EnactedLegislation/Statutes/PDF/ByChapter/Chapter_1A.pdf"},{"n":"35","t":"Physical and mental examination","s":"On motion for good cause, a judge may order an examination “by a physician” when condition is in controversy; requesting the report waives privilege as to others who examined the same condition.","u":"https://www.ncleg.gov/EnactedLegislation/Statutes/PDF/ByChapter/Chapter_1A.pdf"},{"n":"36","t":"Requests for admission","s":"Matters are admitted unless answered or objected to within 30 days, but a defendant need not respond before 60 days after service of summons and complaint. Requests may be served with the summons (the summons must say so). Admissions are conclusive unless withdrawn on motion.","u":"https://www.ncleg.gov/EnactedLegislation/Statutes/PDF/ByChapter/Chapter_1A.pdf"},{"n":"37","t":"Failure to make discovery; sanctions","s":"Motions to compel require a good-faith conference certification; expenses follow the loser unless substantially justified. Sanctions for violating orders include deemed facts, preclusion, striking pleadings, dismissal, default, and contempt. ESI lost through routine good-faith operation is protected absent exceptional circumstances (no federal Rule 37(e) framework). Expenses may be shifted for failure to admit and for failure to participate in a Rule 26(f) discovery plan.","u":"https://www.ncleg.gov/EnactedLegislation/Statutes/PDF/ByChapter/Chapter_1A.pdf"},{"n":"38","t":"Jury trial of right","s":"Demand a jury in writing no later than 10 days after service of the last pleading directed to the issue (federal: 14 days); failure to serve and file the demand waives the jury. A demand cannot be withdrawn without consent of the parties who have appeared. Jury trial is guaranteed on just compensation in condemnation.","u":"https://www.ncleg.gov/EnactedLegislation/Statutes/PDF/ByChapter/Chapter_1A.pdf"},{"n":"39","t":"Trial by jury or by the court","s":"Demanded issues are tried to a jury unless the parties stipulate otherwise or no right exists; the court may order a jury despite no demand, or use an advisory jury.","u":"https://www.ncleg.gov/EnactedLegislation/Statutes/PDF/ByChapter/Chapter_1A.pdf"},{"n":"40","t":"Assignment of cases for trial; continuances","s":"Senior resident superior court judges set calendaring rules; district court calendaring follows G.S. 7A-146. Continuances only for good cause, and must be granted for General Assembly service and certain appointed state boards.","u":"https://www.ncleg.gov/EnactedLegislation/Statutes/PDF/ByChapter/Chapter_1A.pdf"},{"n":"41","t":"Dismissal of actions","s":"A plaintiff may dismiss by notice at any time before resting (federal: before an answer or summary judgment motion) or by stipulation; a second notice dismissal of the same claim is an adjudication on the merits. After a timely action is dismissed without prejudice, a new action on the same claim may be filed within one year (a key savings rule). A plaintiff who dismisses pays the costs, and a refiled case is stayed until prior costs are paid within 30 days of an order.","u":"https://www.ncleg.gov/EnactedLegislation/Statutes/PDF/ByChapter/Chapter_1A.pdf"},{"n":"42","t":"Consolidation; separate trials","s":"Courts may consolidate and must, on motion, bifurcate liability and damages in tort cases seeking over $150,000 unless good cause is shown. Facial challenges to an act of the General Assembly must be transferred to a three-judge panel of Wake County Superior Court (G.S. 1-267.1); a defendant may raise such a challenge within 30 days after filing its answer.","u":"https://www.ncleg.gov/EnactedLegislation/Statutes/PDF/ByChapter/Chapter_1A.pdf"},{"n":"43","t":"Evidence","s":"Testimony is taken orally in open court; adverse parties and their agents may be called and led. Motions on facts outside the record may be heard on affidavits.","u":"https://www.ncleg.gov/EnactedLegislation/Statutes/PDF/ByChapter/Chapter_1A.pdf"},{"n":"44","t":"Proof of official record","s":"Official records are proved by official publication or attested copy with a custody certificate.","u":"https://www.ncleg.gov/EnactedLegislation/Statutes/PDF/ByChapter/Chapter_1A.pdf"},{"n":"44.1","t":"Determination of foreign law","s":"A party must give written notice of an issue of foreign-country law; the court may consider any relevant source, and its ruling is one of law.","u":"https://www.ncleg.gov/EnactedLegislation/Statutes/PDF/ByChapter/Chapter_1A.pdf"},{"n":"45","t":"Subpoena","s":"Any attorney (as officer of the court), judge, magistrate, or the clerk may issue a subpoena; a copy must be served on every party under Rule 5(b). Written objections or a motion to quash are due within 10 days after service (federal: 14 days) or before the compliance date if sooner; an objection stops compliance until a court orders otherwise. The issuing party must notify all parties within 5 business days after receiving subpoenaed material. Out-of-state discovery goes through the other state’s procedures, with a commission from the North Carolina court if needed (objections within 10 days; hearing within 20 days).","u":"https://www.ncleg.gov/EnactedLegislation/Statutes/PDF/ByChapter/Chapter_1A.pdf"},{"n":"46","t":"Objections","s":"Formal exceptions are unnecessary. Continuing objections are deemed for a challenged witness or line of questioning, and rulings on non-evidence matters are preserved by making the party’s position and grounds known.","u":"https://www.ncleg.gov/EnactedLegislation/Statutes/PDF/ByChapter/Chapter_1A.pdf"},{"n":"47","t":"Jurors","s":"Juror qualification and challenges follow Chapter 9 of the General Statutes.","u":"https://www.ncleg.gov/EnactedLegislation/Statutes/PDF/ByChapter/Chapter_1A.pdf"},{"n":"48","t":"Juries of less than twelve","s":"Parties may stipulate to fewer than 12 jurors or to a stated majority verdict, except where a statute requires a jury.","u":"https://www.ncleg.gov/EnactedLegislation/Statutes/PDF/ByChapter/Chapter_1A.pdf"},{"n":"49","t":"Verdicts","s":"General and special verdicts; omitted issues are waived unless submission is demanded before the jury retires.","u":"https://www.ncleg.gov/EnactedLegislation/Statutes/PDF/ByChapter/Chapter_1A.pdf"},{"n":"50","t":"Directed verdict and judgment notwithstanding the verdict","s":"North Carolina keeps the older names: “directed verdict” and “JNOV.” A JNOV motion is due within 10 days after entry of judgment (federal: 28 days), and only a party who moved for directed verdict at the close of all the evidence may make it; the judge may act on its own within the same 10 days. An appellate court cannot direct judgment unless the JNOV motion was made or ruled on.","u":"https://www.ncleg.gov/EnactedLegislation/Statutes/PDF/ByChapter/Chapter_1A.pdf"},{"n":"51","t":"Instructions to jury","s":"The judge may not express an opinion on the facts and need not summarize the evidence; special instruction requests must be written and submitted before the charge. In medical malpractice cases, the instructions must be reduced to writing.","u":"https://www.ncleg.gov/EnactedLegislation/Statutes/PDF/ByChapter/Chapter_1A.pdf"},{"n":"52","t":"Findings by the court","s":"In bench trials the court must find facts specially and state conclusions separately; findings on motions are required only when requested. A motion to amend findings is due within 10 days after entry of judgment (federal: 28 days). Sufficiency of evidence for findings may be raised on appeal without a trial-court objection.","u":"https://www.ncleg.gov/EnactedLegislation/Statutes/PDF/ByChapter/Chapter_1A.pdf"},{"n":"53","t":"Referees","s":"Reference by consent waives jury trial on referred issues; a compulsory reference preserves it only if the party objects when ordered, files specific exceptions within 30 days after the report is filed, and tenders issues and a jury demand. Exceptions to a referee’s report are due within 30 days of filing.","u":"https://www.ncleg.gov/EnactedLegislation/Statutes/PDF/ByChapter/Chapter_1A.pdf"},{"n":"54","t":"Judgments","s":"A partial final judgment requires a determination of “no just reason for delay”; otherwise orders on fewer than all claims or parties are not appealable except as statutes allow (in North Carolina, a “substantial right” order may still be appealable under G.S. 1-277 and 7A-27). Default judgments cannot exceed the demand.","u":"https://www.ncleg.gov/EnactedLegislation/Statutes/PDF/ByChapter/Chapter_1A.pdf"},{"n":"55","t":"Default","s":"The clerk enters default and may enter judgment for a sum certain against a non-appearing, competent defendant. A defendant who has appeared must get written notice of a default-judgment application at least 3 days before the hearing (federal: 7 days). A motion may be decided without a hearing if it says so and no written response is served within 30 days. Entry of default may be set aside for good cause; default judgment under Rule 60(b).","u":"https://www.ncleg.gov/EnactedLegislation/Statutes/PDF/ByChapter/Chapter_1A.pdf"},{"n":"56","t":"Summary judgment","s":"A claimant may move after 30 days from commencement; a defending party at any time. The motion must be served at least 10 days before the hearing and opposing affidavits at least 2 days before. Bad-faith affidavits trigger mandatory expense awards.","u":"https://www.ncleg.gov/EnactedLegislation/Statutes/PDF/ByChapter/Chapter_1A.pdf"},{"n":"57","t":"Declaratory judgments","s":"Procedure under Article 26 of Chapter 1 follows these rules, with jury trial available under Rules 38 and 39; another adequate remedy does not bar declaratory relief.","u":"https://www.ncleg.gov/EnactedLegislation/Statutes/PDF/ByChapter/Chapter_1A.pdf"},{"n":"58","t":"Entry of judgment","s":"A judgment is entered when it is reduced to writing, signed by the judge, and filed with the clerk. The designated party (or the drafter) must serve it on all parties within 3 days; while it is not served, the Rule 50(b), 52(b), and 59 periods are tolled, but not more than 90 days from entry. Add 3 days if served by mail.","u":"https://www.ncleg.gov/EnactedLegislation/Statutes/PDF/ByChapter/Chapter_1A.pdf"},{"n":"59","t":"New trials; amendment of judgments","s":"A new-trial motion and a motion to alter or amend are due within 10 days after entry of judgment (federal: 28 days); the court may act on its own within the same 10 days. Opposing affidavits are due within 10 days after service, extendable up to 30 more days. The listed grounds include ‘any other reason heretofore recognized.’","u":"https://www.ncleg.gov/EnactedLegislation/Statutes/PDF/ByChapter/Chapter_1A.pdf"},{"n":"60","t":"Relief from judgment or order","s":"Clerical mistakes may be corrected anytime (with appellate leave after the appeal is docketed). Rule 60(b)(1)–(3) motions must be made within one year; all within a reasonable time. A Rule 60(b) motion does not suspend the judgment.","u":"https://www.ncleg.gov/EnactedLegislation/Statutes/PDF/ByChapter/Chapter_1A.pdf"},{"n":"61","t":"Harmless error","s":"Errors are disregarded unless refusing relief denies a substantial right.","u":"https://www.ncleg.gov/EnactedLegislation/Statutes/PDF/ByChapter/Chapter_1A.pdf"},{"n":"62","t":"Stay of proceedings to enforce a judgment","s":"No execution until the time for noticing an appeal expires, except injunction and receivership judgments, which are not stayed unless ordered. A stay on appeal follows G.S. 1-289 to 1-295 (supersedeas bond, capped at $25 million for judgments of $25 million or more under G.S. 1-289(b)); the State and local governments need no bond. Rule 62(h) gives an immediate appeal of orders granting or denying relief against enforcement of an act of the General Assembly as applied.","u":"https://www.ncleg.gov/EnactedLegislation/Statutes/PDF/ByChapter/Chapter_1A.pdf"},{"n":"63","t":"Disability of a judge","s":"If the trial judge cannot act after verdict or hearing, the senior resident superior court judge (or chief district judge) may enter judgment or, if unable to perform, order a new trial.","u":"https://www.ncleg.gov/EnactedLegislation/Statutes/PDF/ByChapter/Chapter_1A.pdf"},{"n":"64","t":"Seizure of person or property","s":"Provisional remedies are available as provided by North Carolina law.","u":"https://www.ncleg.gov/EnactedLegislation/Statutes/PDF/ByChapter/Chapter_1A.pdf"},{"n":"65","t":"Injunctions","s":"No preliminary injunction without notice. An ex parte TRO expires in no more than 10 days (federal: 14), extendable once for a like period for good cause; the adverse party may move to dissolve on 2 days’ notice. Security is required except from the State and local governments, and in spousal suits restraining molestation.","u":"https://www.ncleg.gov/EnactedLegislation/Statutes/PDF/ByChapter/Chapter_1A.pdf"},{"n":"66–67","t":"Omitted","s":"Rules 66 and 67 are omitted from the North Carolina rules.","u":"https://www.ncleg.gov/EnactedLegislation/Statutes/PDF/ByChapter/Chapter_1A.pdf"},{"n":"68","t":"Offer of judgment and disclaimer","s":"A defending party may serve an offer more than 10 days before trial; acceptance is due within 10 days (federal: 14). If the offeree does no better, it pays costs incurred after the offer. Rule 68(b) lets a contract defendant offer, with its answer, a stipulated damages figure that the claimant may accept within 20 days.","u":"https://www.ncleg.gov/EnactedLegislation/Statutes/PDF/ByChapter/Chapter_1A.pdf"},{"n":"68.1","t":"Confession of judgment","s":"A prospective defendant may file a verified statement with the clerk authorizing judgment for money due or to become due (including alimony or child support), in the county of the defendant’s residence or real property or the plaintiff’s residence; it is not res judicata on facts except in an action on the judgment.","u":"https://www.ncleg.gov/EnactedLegislation/Statutes/PDF/ByChapter/Chapter_1A.pdf"},{"n":"69","t":"Omitted","s":"Rule 69 is omitted; execution is governed by Chapter 1 of the General Statutes.","u":"https://www.ncleg.gov/EnactedLegislation/Statutes/PDF/ByChapter/Chapter_1A.pdf"},{"n":"70","t":"Judgment for specific acts; vesting title","s":"The court may appoint someone to perform an act a party refuses to do, issue attachment or sequestration, hold the party in contempt, or vest title to in-state property directly.","u":"https://www.ncleg.gov/EnactedLegislation/Statutes/PDF/ByChapter/Chapter_1A.pdf"},{"n":"71–83","t":"Omitted","s":"Rules 71 through 83 are omitted from the North Carolina rules.","u":"https://www.ncleg.gov/EnactedLegislation/Statutes/PDF/ByChapter/Chapter_1A.pdf"},{"n":"84","t":"Forms","s":"Sample complaints, motions, and answers illustrate the brevity the rules contemplate.","u":"https://www.ncleg.gov/EnactedLegislation/Statutes/PDF/ByChapter/Chapter_1A.pdf"}]});
